WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820
Start with the net result. The proton proton

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:06.440
chain turns hydrogen into helium. The illustration combines

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.660
four hydrogen nuclei into one helium nucleus, with

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.860
energy and other products leaving. It summarizes the

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.520
net reaction rather than every intermediate step.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.280
The C N O cycle also turns hydrogen

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:22.540
into helium. Carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen participate in

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:25.720
intermediate steps. The catalyst cycle returns to its

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.580
starting nucleus while hydrogen has been consumed and

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:31.760
helium released. Hotter cores favor this route more

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:32.280
strongly.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.660
Triple alpha has a different fuel. Two helium

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:39.480
nuclei briefly form beryllium; adding a third makes

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.800
carbon. Some carbon captures another helium nucleus to

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.280
make oxygen. In the lecture’s local approximations, triple

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.950
alpha is more temperature sensitive than C N

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.280
O, which is more sensitive than the proton

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.260
proton chain.
